-- Calculus
insert into public.packs (id, category_id, title, course, description, position) values
  ('calc-limits', 'calculus', 'Limits & Continuity', 'Calculus I', 'One-sided, infinite and at-infinity limits, trig limits, squeeze theorem, indeterminate forms, L''Hôpital, continuity, discontinuities, IVT and ε-δ.', 0),
  ('calc-derivatives', 'calculus', 'Derivative Rules', 'Calculus I', 'Limit definition, power/product/quotient/chain rules, trig, exponential, log and inverse trig derivatives, implicit and logarithmic differentiation.', 1),
  ('calc-derivative-apps', 'calculus', 'Applications of Derivatives', 'Calculus I', 'Critical numbers, EVT and the Closed Interval Method, Rolle and MVT, derivative tests, concavity, linearization, related rates, optimization, Newton''s method.', 2),
  ('calc-integration', 'calculus', 'Integration Techniques', 'Calculus II', 'FTC parts 1 and 2, key antiderivatives, u-substitution, by parts, trig integrals and substitution, partial fractions, improper integrals, area and volume.', 3),
  ('calc-series', 'calculus', 'Sequences & Series', 'Calculus II', 'Geometric and p-series, divergence, integral, comparison, alternating and ratio tests, intervals of convergence, Taylor and Maclaurin series, remainders.', 4),
  ('calc-multivariable', 'calculus', 'Multivariable & Vector Calculus', 'Calculus III', 'Cross products, partials, gradients, tangent planes, 2D extrema, Lagrange multipliers, polar/spherical integrals, Jacobians, Green, Stokes, divergence.', 5)
on conflict (id) do update set category_id = excluded.category_id, title = excluded.title, course = excluded.course, description = excluded.description, position = excluded.position;

insert into public.pack_cards (id, pack_id, position, title, body, answer, accepted_answers) values
  ('calc-limits/two-sided-limit-exists', 'calc-limits', 0, 'When does the two-sided limit lim(x→a) f(x) exist?', 'Exactly when both one-sided limits exist and are equal: lim(x→a⁻) f(x) = lim(x→a⁺) f(x) = L.
The value f(a) is irrelevant: it can be undefined or different from L without changing the limit.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-limits/one-sided-infinite-limit', 'calc-limits', 1, 'lim(x→3⁻) 1/(x − 3) = ?', '−∞.
Just left of 3, x − 3 is a tiny negative number, so 1/(x − 3) is hugely negative. From the right it''s +∞, so the two-sided limit does not exist and x = 3 is a vertical asymptote.', '-inf', array['negative infinity']::text[]),
  ('calc-limits/rationalize-zero-over-zero', 'calc-limits', 2, 'Evaluate lim(h→0) (√(9 + h) − 3)/h.', '1/6.
Substitution gives 0/0, so multiply top and bottom by the conjugate √(9 + h) + 3: the numerator becomes h, leaving 1/(√(9 + h) + 3) → 1/6. It''s also the derivative of √x at x = 9.', '1/6', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-limits/squeeze-theorem', 'calc-limits', 3, 'Use the squeeze theorem: lim(x→0) x² sin(1/x) = ?', '0.
−1 ≤ sin(1/x) ≤ 1, so −x² ≤ x² sin(1/x) ≤ x², and both bounds → 0. Theorem: if g ≤ f ≤ h near a and lim g = lim h = L, then lim f = L. The product law fails here because lim sin(1/x) doesn''t exist.', '0', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-limits/sin-x-over-x', 'calc-limits', 4, 'lim(x→0) (sin x)/x = ?', '1 (x in radians).
It follows from the squeeze cos x ≤ (sin x)/x ≤ 1 near 0. Rescale for variants: lim(x→0) sin(5x)/x = 5·lim sin(5x)/(5x) = 5.', '1', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-limits/one-minus-cos-over-x', 'calc-limits', 5, 'lim(x→0) (1 − cos x)/x = ?', '0.
Multiply by (1 + cos x)/(1 + cos x) to get (sin x/x)·(sin x/(1 + cos x)) → 1·0 = 0. Don''t confuse it with lim(x→0) (1 − cos x)/x² = 1/2.', '0', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-limits/rational-limit-at-infinity', 'calc-limits', 6, 'lim(x→∞) (3x² − x + 1)/(5x² + 4) = ?', '3/5.
Equal degrees: the ratio of leading coefficients (divide every term by x²). Top degree lower: the limit is 0. Top degree higher: ±∞, so no horizontal asymptote.', '3/5', array['0.6', '.6']::text[]),
  ('calc-limits/continuity-three-conditions', 'calc-limits', 7, 'f is continuous at x = a when which three conditions all hold?', '1) f(a) is defined. 2) lim(x→a) f(x) exists. 3) lim(x→a) f(x) = f(a).
If any one fails, f is discontinuous at a. Condition 3 is the one people skip: the limit must exist and also equal the function value.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-limits/removable-discontinuity', 'calc-limits', 8, 'What type of discontinuity does f(x) = (x² − x − 2)/(x − 2) have at x = 2?', 'Removable.
It factors to (x − 2)(x + 1)/(x − 2), so the limit is 3 but f(2) is undefined; defining f(2) = 3 repairs it. Jump: one-sided limits exist but differ. Infinite: f → ±∞ (vertical asymptote).', 'removable', array['removable discontinuity', 'hole']::text[]),
  ('calc-limits/ivt-hypothesis', 'calc-limits', 9, 'The Intermediate Value Theorem needs f to be ___ on the closed interval [a, b].', 'Continuous.
Then f takes every value N between f(a) and f(b) at some c in (a, b). Classic use: x³ + x − 1 is −1 at x = 0 and 1 at x = 1, so it has a root in (0, 1). It proves a root exists, not exactly where or how many.', 'continuous', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-limits/indeterminate-forms', 'calc-limits', 10, 'List the seven indeterminate forms.', '0/0, ∞/∞, 0·∞, ∞ − ∞, 1^∞, 0⁰, ∞⁰.
Not indeterminate: 0^∞ → 0, ∞ + ∞ → ∞, ∞·∞ → ∞. Before L''Hôpital, rewrite products and differences as quotients, and handle powers by taking ln.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-limits/lhopital-conditions', 'calc-limits', 11, 'L''Hôpital''s rule: what must be true before you replace lim f(x)/g(x) with lim f′(x)/g′(x)?', 'The limit must be type 0/0 or ±∞/±∞; f and g differentiable near a with g′(x) ≠ 0 (except possibly at a); and lim f′/g′ must exist or be ±∞.
It''s f′/g′, not the quotient rule. Trap: lim(x→1) (x + 1)/x = 2 is not indeterminate, yet the rule would give 1.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-limits/limit-x-to-the-x', 'calc-limits', 12, 'lim(x→0⁺) xˣ = ?', '1.
0⁰ form: let y = xˣ, so ln y = x ln x = (ln x)/(1/x), type −∞/∞. L''Hôpital: (1/x)/(−1/x²) = −x → 0. So ln y → 0 and y → e⁰ = 1.', '1', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-limits/limit-definition-of-e', 'calc-limits', 13, 'lim(n→∞) (1 + 1/n)ⁿ = ?', 'e ≈ 2.71828.
It''s a 1^∞ form, not 1: its ln is n ln(1 + 1/n) → 1, so the limit is e¹. More generally, lim(n→∞) (1 + a/n)ⁿ = eᵃ.', 'e', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-limits/epsilon-delta-definition', 'calc-limits', 14, 'Precise definition: lim(x→a) f(x) = L means for every ε > 0 there is a δ > 0 such that ___.', 'If 0 < |x − a| < δ, then |f(x) − L| < ε.
ε comes first and δ may depend on it; 0 < |x − a| excludes x = a itself. Example: for lim(x→2) (4x − 1) = 7, |(4x − 1) − 7| = 4|x − 2|, so δ = ε/4 works.', null, '{}'::text[])
on conflict (id) do update set pack_id = excluded.pack_id, position = excluded.position, title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, answer = excluded.answer, accepted_answers = excluded.accepted_answers;
delete from public.pack_cards where pack_id = 'calc-limits' and id not in ('calc-limits/two-sided-limit-exists', 'calc-limits/one-sided-infinite-limit', 'calc-limits/rationalize-zero-over-zero', 'calc-limits/squeeze-theorem', 'calc-limits/sin-x-over-x', 'calc-limits/one-minus-cos-over-x', 'calc-limits/rational-limit-at-infinity', 'calc-limits/continuity-three-conditions', 'calc-limits/removable-discontinuity', 'calc-limits/ivt-hypothesis', 'calc-limits/indeterminate-forms', 'calc-limits/lhopital-conditions', 'calc-limits/limit-x-to-the-x', 'calc-limits/limit-definition-of-e', 'calc-limits/epsilon-delta-definition');

insert into public.pack_cards (id, pack_id, position, title, body, answer, accepted_answers) values
  ('calc-derivatives/limit-definition-of-derivative', 'calc-derivatives', 0, 'Write the limit definition of the derivative f′(x).', 'f′(x) = lim(h→0) [f(x + h) − f(x)]/h, if the limit exists.
It''s the limit of secant slopes, i.e. the slope of the tangent line. Equivalent form at a point: f′(a) = lim(x→a) [f(x) − f(a)]/(x − a).', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/differentiable-vs-continuous', 'calc-derivatives', 1, 'Differentiable at a implies continuous at a. Is the converse true?', 'No. f(x) = |x| is continuous at 0 but has a corner there: slope −1 from the left, +1 from the right, so f′(0) does not exist.
f can also fail to be differentiable at a vertical tangent (∛x at 0) or at a discontinuity.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/derivative-of-sqrt-x', 'calc-derivatives', 2, 'd/dx [√x] = ?', '1/(2√x).
Rewrite √x = x^(1/2) and use the power rule d/dx [xⁿ] = nxⁿ⁻¹ (valid for any real n): ½x^(−1/2). Same trick: d/dx [1/x] = d/dx [x⁻¹] = −1/x².', '1/(2sqrt(x))', array['1/(2sqrt x)', '(1/2)x^(-1/2)', '1/2x^(-1/2)', '0.5x^(-0.5)', '0.5x^-0.5', 'x^(-1/2)/2', '1/(2x^(1/2))', '1/(2x^0.5)']::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/product-rule', 'calc-derivatives', 3, 'Product rule: d/dx [f(x)g(x)] = ?', 'f′(x)g(x) + f(x)g′(x).
Example: d/dx [x² sin x] = 2x sin x + x² cos x. Trap: the derivative of a product is not the product of the derivatives, f′(x)g′(x).', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/quotient-rule', 'calc-derivatives', 4, 'Quotient rule: d/dx [f(x)/g(x)] = ?', '[g(x)f′(x) − f(x)g′(x)]/[g(x)]².
Mnemonic: low d-high minus high d-low, over the square of what''s below. Order matters because of the minus. Check: d/dx [1/x] = (x·0 − 1·1)/x² = −1/x².', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/derivative-of-tan', 'calc-derivatives', 5, 'd/dx [tan x] = ?', 'sec²x.
Quotient rule on sin x/cos x: [cos x·cos x − sin x·(−sin x)]/cos²x = 1/cos²x, since sin²x + cos²x = 1.', 'sec^2 x', array['sec^2(x)', 'sec(x)^2', '(sec x)^2', '1/cos^2 x', '1/cos^2(x)', '1/(cos x)^2']::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/derivative-of-sec', 'calc-derivatives', 6, 'd/dx [sec x] = ?', 'sec x tan x.
Quotient rule on 1/cos x: [cos x·0 − 1·(−sin x)]/cos²x = sin x/cos²x = (1/cos x)(sin x/cos x).', 'sec x tan x', array['sec(x)tan(x)', 'tan x sec x', 'tan(x)sec(x)']::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/co-function-derivative-signs', 'calc-derivatives', 7, 'Which three of the six trig functions have derivatives that carry a minus sign?', 'cos, cot and csc (the co- functions): d/dx [cos x] = −sin x, d/dx [cot x] = −csc²x, d/dx [csc x] = −csc x cot x.
Pattern: take the partner''s derivative, swap in co-functions, add a minus. tan → sec² becomes cot → −csc²; sec → sec tan becomes csc → −csc cot.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/chain-rule', 'calc-derivatives', 8, 'Chain rule: d/dx [f(g(x))] = ?', 'f′(g(x))·g′(x): the derivative of the outside, evaluated at the inside, times the derivative of the inside.
Example: d/dx [sin(x²)] = cos(x²)·2x. Trap: forgetting the inner factor 2x.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/derivative-of-a-to-the-x', 'calc-derivatives', 9, 'd/dx [aˣ] = ? (constant a > 0)', 'aˣ ln a.
Write aˣ = e^(x ln a) and apply the chain rule. Example: d/dx [2ˣ] = 2ˣ ln 2. Trap: x·aˣ⁻¹ is the power rule, which needs a variable base and a constant exponent.', 'a^x ln a', array['a^x ln(a)', 'a^x(ln a)', 'ln(a) a^x', '(ln a)a^x', 'ln a * a^x']::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/implicit-differentiation-circle', 'calc-derivatives', 10, 'Find dy/dx by implicit differentiation: x² + y² = 25.', 'dy/dx = −x/y.
2x + 2y·(dy/dx) = 0, so dy/dx = −x/y; at (3, 4) the tangent slope is −3/4. Trap: dropping the dy/dx that the chain rule attaches to y terms: d/dx [y²] = 2y·dy/dx, not 2y.', '-x/y', array['-(x/y)', 'dy/dx=-x/y', 'y''=-x/y']::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/derivative-of-arcsin', 'calc-derivatives', 11, 'd/dx [arcsin x] = ?', '1/√(1 − x²), for −1 < x < 1.
sin y = x ⇒ cos y·y′ = 1 ⇒ y′ = 1/cos y = 1/√(1 − x²), since cos y ≥ 0 for y in [−π/2, π/2]. arccos x has the same derivative with a minus sign.', '1/sqrt(1-x^2)', array['(1-x^2)^(-1/2)', '1/(sqrt(1-x^2))', '1/sqrt(-x^2+1)']::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/derivative-of-arctan', 'calc-derivatives', 12, 'd/dx [arctan x] = ?', '1/(1 + x²), for all real x.
tan y = x ⇒ sec²y·y′ = 1 ⇒ y′ = 1/(1 + tan²y) = 1/(1 + x²). Read in reverse: ∫ dx/(1 + x²) = arctan x + C.', '1/(1+x^2)', array['1/(x^2+1)', '(1+x^2)^(-1)', '(x^2+1)^(-1)']::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/derivative-of-inverse-function', 'calc-derivatives', 13, 'f(x) = x³ + x, so f(1) = 2. Find (f⁻¹)′(2).', '1/4.
(f⁻¹)′(b) = 1/f′(f⁻¹(b)). Here f⁻¹(2) = 1 and f′(x) = 3x² + 1, so f′(1) = 4. Trap: evaluating f′ at 2 instead of at f⁻¹(2) = 1 gives 1/13.', '1/4', array['0.25', '.25']::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/derivative-of-log-base-a', 'calc-derivatives', 14, 'd/dx [logₐx] = ? (a > 0, a ≠ 1)', '1/(x ln a).
Change of base: logₐx = (ln x)/(ln a), and 1/ln a is just a constant factor. With a = e this is d/dx [ln x] = 1/x. Example: d/dx [log₁₀x] = 1/(x ln 10).', '1/(x ln a)', array['1/(x ln(a))', '1/(ln(a) x)', '1/(ln a * x)', '(1/ln a)(1/x)']::text[]),
  ('calc-derivatives/logarithmic-differentiation', 'calc-derivatives', 15, 'Use logarithmic differentiation: d/dx [xˣ] = ? (x > 0)', 'xˣ(ln x + 1).
ln y = x ln x ⇒ y′/y = ln x + 1 ⇒ y′ = xˣ(ln x + 1). Neither the power rule (x·xˣ⁻¹) nor the exponential rule (xˣ ln x) applies: base and exponent both vary.', 'x^x(ln x + 1)', array['x^x(1 + ln x)', 'x^x(ln(x)+1)', 'x^x(1+ln(x))', '(ln x + 1)x^x', '(1 + ln x)x^x', 'x^x ln x + x^x', 'x^x + x^x ln x']::text[])
on conflict (id) do update set pack_id = excluded.pack_id, position = excluded.position, title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, answer = excluded.answer, accepted_answers = excluded.accepted_answers;
delete from public.pack_cards where pack_id = 'calc-derivatives' and id not in ('calc-derivatives/limit-definition-of-derivative', 'calc-derivatives/differentiable-vs-continuous', 'calc-derivatives/derivative-of-sqrt-x', 'calc-derivatives/product-rule', 'calc-derivatives/quotient-rule', 'calc-derivatives/derivative-of-tan', 'calc-derivatives/derivative-of-sec', 'calc-derivatives/co-function-derivative-signs', 'calc-derivatives/chain-rule', 'calc-derivatives/derivative-of-a-to-the-x', 'calc-derivatives/implicit-differentiation-circle', 'calc-derivatives/derivative-of-arcsin', 'calc-derivatives/derivative-of-arctan', 'calc-derivatives/derivative-of-inverse-function', 'calc-derivatives/derivative-of-log-base-a', 'calc-derivatives/logarithmic-differentiation');

insert into public.pack_cards (id, pack_id, position, title, body, answer, accepted_answers) values
  ('calc-derivative-apps/critical-numbers', 'calc-derivative-apps', 0, 'What makes c a critical number of f?', 'c is in the domain of f, and either f′(c) = 0 or f′(c) does not exist.
Example: x^(2/3) has a critical number at 0, where f′ is undefined. Fermat: local extrema occur only at critical numbers, but not every critical number is one (x³ at 0).', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivative-apps/extreme-value-theorem', 'calc-derivative-apps', 1, 'Extreme Value Theorem: what hypotheses guarantee f attains an absolute max and an absolute min?', 'f is continuous on a closed interval [a, b].
Then f(c) is the absolute max and f(d) the absolute min for some c, d in [a, b]. Drop either condition and it can fail: x on (0, 1) has neither (not closed); 1/x on [−1, 1] with f(0) = 0 has neither (not continuous).', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivative-apps/closed-interval-method', 'calc-derivative-apps', 2, 'What is the absolute maximum value of f(x) = x³ − 3x on [0, 3]?', '18, at the endpoint x = 3.
Closed Interval Method: f′ = 3x² − 3 = 0 at x = ±1, and only x = 1 lies in [0, 3]. Compare f(0) = 0, f(1) = −2, f(3) = 18: the largest is the absolute max, the smallest (−2) the absolute min. Skip the endpoints and you miss the max.', '18', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivative-apps/rolles-theorem', 'calc-derivative-apps', 3, 'Rolle''s Theorem: f is continuous on [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b). What third hypothesis is needed?', 'f(a) = f(b).
Then f′(c) = 0 for some c in (a, b): a horizontal tangent. Differentiability matters: |x| on [−1, 1] has equal endpoint values but no horizontal tangent.', 'f(a)=f(b)', array['f(b)=f(a)']::text[]),
  ('calc-derivative-apps/mean-value-theorem', 'calc-derivative-apps', 4, 'Mean Value Theorem: f is continuous on [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b). What does it guarantee?', 'Some c in (a, b) has f′(c) = [f(b) − f(a)]/(b − a): instantaneous rate = average rate, so some tangent is parallel to the secant.
Example: f(x) = x² on [0, 2] has average slope 2, and f′(c) = 2c = 2 at c = 1. Rolle''s Theorem is the case f(a) = f(b).', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivative-apps/mvt-bound', 'calc-derivative-apps', 5, 'f(0) = −3 and f′(x) ≤ 5 for all x. What is the largest possible value of f(2)?', '7.
MVT on [0, 2]: f(2) − f(0) = 2f′(c) ≤ 10, so f(2) ≤ −3 + 10 = 7. The bound is reached by f(x) = 5x − 3.', '7', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivative-apps/first-derivative-test', 'calc-derivative-apps', 6, 'First Derivative Test: f′ changes from positive to negative at a critical number c. What does f have at c?', 'A local maximum (rising, then falling).
Negative to positive gives a local min; no sign change (x³ at 0) gives neither. It needs only the sign of f′ near c, so it also works at corners where f′(c) doesn''t exist.', 'local max', array['local maximum', 'relative max', 'relative maximum']::text[]),
  ('calc-derivative-apps/second-derivative-test', 'calc-derivative-apps', 7, 'Second Derivative Test: f′(c) = 0. What do f″(c) > 0, f″(c) < 0 and f″(c) = 0 each tell you?', 'f″(c) > 0: local min (concave up). f″(c) < 0: local max (concave down). f″(c) = 0: inconclusive.
x⁴, −x⁴ and x³ all have f′(0) = f″(0) = 0, yet give a min, a max and neither. When it''s inconclusive, fall back on the First Derivative Test.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivative-apps/inflection-point-check', 'calc-derivative-apps', 8, 'f(x) = x⁴ has f″(0) = 0. Does f have an inflection point at x = 0?', 'No. f″(x) = 12x² is positive on both sides of 0, so the graph is concave up throughout.
An inflection point needs a change in concavity (f″ changes sign). f″ = 0 only flags a candidate.', 'no', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivative-apps/linearization', 'calc-derivative-apps', 9, 'Linearization of f at x = a: L(x) = ?', 'L(x) = f(a) + f′(a)(x − a), the tangent line at a.
Example: √x at a = 4 gives L(x) = 2 + (x − 4)/4, so √4.1 ≈ 2.025 (true value ≈ 2.0248). Accurate only near a.', 'f(a)+f''(a)(x-a)', array['f''(a)(x-a)+f(a)', 'f(a)+(x-a)f''(a)', 'L(x)=f(a)+f''(a)(x-a)']::text[]),
  ('calc-derivative-apps/differentials', 'calc-derivative-apps', 10, 'For y = f(x), what is the differential dy, and how does it relate to Δy?', 'dy = f′(x) dx, the change along the tangent line. Δy = f(x + Δx) − f(x) is the actual change, and Δy ≈ dy when dx = Δx is small.
Use: error estimates. For a sphere, V = (4/3)πr³ gives dV = 4πr² dr.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivative-apps/related-rates-circle', 'calc-derivative-apps', 11, 'A circle''s radius grows at 2 cm/s. How fast is its area growing when r = 5 cm?', '20π cm²/s (about 62.8).
A = πr², so dA/dt = 2πr·dr/dt = 2π(5)(2). Strategy: write an equation linking the quantities, differentiate with respect to t, and only then plug in the values at that instant.', '20pi', array['20pi cm^2/s', '20pi cm^2/sec']::text[]),
  ('calc-derivative-apps/optimization-rectangle', 'calc-derivative-apps', 12, 'Of all rectangles with perimeter 40, what is the largest possible area?', '100, from a 10 by 10 square.
A = x(20 − x) on [0, 20]; A′ = 20 − 2x = 0 at x = 10, and the endpoints give A = 0. Strategy: write the objective in one variable, find its domain, then compare critical points and endpoints.', '100', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-derivative-apps/newtons-method', 'calc-derivative-apps', 13, 'Newton''s method on f(x) = x² − 2 with x₁ = 1: what is x₂?', '1.5.
xₙ₊₁ = xₙ − f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ) = 1 − (−1)/2 = 1.5. Next, x₃ ≈ 1.4167, closing in on √2 ≈ 1.4142. It can fail if f′(xₙ) = 0 or the first guess is poor.', '1.5', array['3/2']::text[]),
  ('calc-derivative-apps/antiderivative-initial-value', 'calc-derivative-apps', 14, 'Find f(x) if f′(x) = 6x² and f(1) = 5.', 'f(x) = 2x³ + 3.
The general antiderivative is 2x³ + C (on an interval, antiderivatives differ only by a constant). Then f(1) = 2 + C = 5 gives C = 3. Forget the + C and the initial condition can''t be met.', '2x^3+3', array['f(x)=2x^3+3', 'y=2x^3+3', '3+2x^3']::text[])
on conflict (id) do update set pack_id = excluded.pack_id, position = excluded.position, title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, answer = excluded.answer, accepted_answers = excluded.accepted_answers;
delete from public.pack_cards where pack_id = 'calc-derivative-apps' and id not in ('calc-derivative-apps/critical-numbers', 'calc-derivative-apps/extreme-value-theorem', 'calc-derivative-apps/closed-interval-method', 'calc-derivative-apps/rolles-theorem', 'calc-derivative-apps/mean-value-theorem', 'calc-derivative-apps/mvt-bound', 'calc-derivative-apps/first-derivative-test', 'calc-derivative-apps/second-derivative-test', 'calc-derivative-apps/inflection-point-check', 'calc-derivative-apps/linearization', 'calc-derivative-apps/differentials', 'calc-derivative-apps/related-rates-circle', 'calc-derivative-apps/optimization-rectangle', 'calc-derivative-apps/newtons-method', 'calc-derivative-apps/antiderivative-initial-value');

insert into public.pack_cards (id, pack_id, position, title, body, answer, accepted_answers) values
  ('calc-integration/ftc-part-2', 'calc-integration', 0, 'FTC Part 2 (net change): ∫ₐᵇ F′(x) dx = ?', 'F(b) − F(a).
Example: ∫₀¹ x² dx = F(1) − F(0) with F(x) = x³/3, so 1/3. It needs F′ continuous on [a, b]: ∫₋₁¹ dx/x² is not −2; it''s improper and diverges.', 'F(b)-F(a)', array['-F(a)+F(b)']::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/ftc-part-1-chain-rule', 'calc-integration', 1, 'd/dx ∫₁^(x²) cos t dt = ?', '2x cos(x²).
FTC Part 1: d/dx ∫ₐˣ f(t) dt = f(x). With upper limit g(x), the chain rule gives f(g(x))·g′(x). A constant lower limit doesn''t matter.', '2x cos(x^2)', array['cos(x^2)*2x', 'cos(x^2)(2x)', '2x cos x^2']::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/integral-of-one-over-x', 'calc-integration', 2, '∫ (1/x) dx = ?', 'ln|x| + C.
The absolute value makes it valid for x < 0 too: d/dx [ln|x|] = 1/x on both sides of 0. It''s the one power the rule ∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + C can''t handle (n = −1).', 'ln|x| + C', array['ln|x|', 'ln(|x|) + C', 'ln(|x|)']::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/arctan-integral-form', 'calc-integration', 3, '∫ dx/(x² + a²) = ? (a > 0)', '(1/a) arctan(x/a) + C.
Check: d/dx [(1/a) arctan(x/a)] = (1/a²)/(1 + x²/a²) = 1/(x² + a²). Example: ∫ dx/(x² + 9) = (1/3) arctan(x/3) + C. Companion: ∫ dx/√(a² − x²) = arcsin(x/a) + C.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/integral-of-tan', 'calc-integration', 4, '∫ tan x dx = ?', 'ln|sec x| + C, equivalently −ln|cos x| + C.
Write tan x = sin x/cos x and let u = cos x, du = −sin x dx: −∫ du/u = −ln|cos x| + C. The forms agree since ln|sec x| = −ln|cos x|.', 'ln|sec x| + C', array['-ln|cos x| + C', 'ln|sec(x)| + C', '-ln|cos(x)| + C', 'ln|sec x|', '-ln|cos x|', 'ln|sec(x)|', '-ln|cos(x)|']::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/u-sub-changing-bounds', 'calc-integration', 5, 'Evaluate ∫₀² 2x(x² + 1)³ dx.', '156.
u = x² + 1, du = 2x dx, and the bounds become u = 1 to u = 5: ∫₁⁵ u³ du = (5⁴ − 1⁴)/4 = 624/4 = 156. Trap: switching to u but keeping the old bounds 0 and 2 gives 4.', '156', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/area-between-curves', 'calc-integration', 6, 'Area of the region between y = x and y = x² for 0 ≤ x ≤ 1?', '1/6.
On [0, 1], x ≥ x², so A = ∫₀¹ (x − x²) dx = 1/2 − 1/3 = 1/6. Always integrate top minus bottom; if the curves cross, split at the crossing (or integrate |f − g|).', '1/6', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/disk-method', 'calc-integration', 7, 'Volume when the region under y = √x, 0 ≤ x ≤ 1, is revolved about the x-axis?', 'π/2.
Disks: V = ∫₀¹ π(√x)² dx = π∫₀¹ x dx = π/2. If the region leaves a hole, use washers: V = ∫ π(R² − r²) dx, not π(R − r)².', 'pi/2', array['(1/2)pi', '0.5pi']::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/shell-method', 'calc-integration', 8, 'Shell method: volume when the region under y = f(x), a ≤ x ≤ b (a ≥ 0), is revolved about the y-axis?', 'V = ∫ₐᵇ 2πx f(x) dx: circumference 2πx times height f(x) times thickness dx.
Shells parallel to the axis of rotation avoid solving y = f(x) for x, which washers about the y-axis would require.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/average-value', 'calc-integration', 9, 'Average value of f(x) = x² on [0, 3]?', '3.
f_avg = (1/(b − a)) ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = (1/3)·9 = 3. Mean Value Theorem for Integrals: f(c) = f_avg for some c in [a, b]; here c = √3.', '3', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/integration-by-parts', 'calc-integration', 10, 'Integration by parts: ∫ u dv = ?', 'uv − ∫ v du (the product rule, integrated).
Example: ∫ x eˣ dx with u = x, dv = eˣ dx gives x eˣ − ∫ eˣ dx = x eˣ − eˣ + C. Choose u to get simpler when differentiated, and dv easy to integrate.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/liate-choice-of-u', 'calc-integration', 11, 'In ∫ x² ln x dx, which factor should be u for integration by parts?', 'u = ln x, dv = x² dx. LIATE (Log, Inverse trig, Algebraic, Trig, Exponential) ranks u choices; ln x is easy to differentiate but awkward to integrate.
Result: (x³/3) ln x − ∫ (x³/3)(1/x) dx = (x³/3) ln x − x³/9 + C.', 'u = ln x', array['ln x', 'ln(x)', 'u=ln(x)']::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/odd-power-of-sine', 'calc-integration', 12, '∫ sin³x cos²x dx: which substitution turns it into the integral of a polynomial?', 'u = cos x.
sin has the odd power: save one sin x for du = −sin x dx and convert sin²x = 1 − cos²x. If cos has the odd power, save a cos x and let u = sin x. Both powers even: use half-angle identities.', 'u = cos x', array['u=cos(x)', 'cos x', 'cos(x)']::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/trig-substitution-table', 'calc-integration', 13, 'Which trig substitution matches √(a² − x²), √(a² + x²) and √(x² − a²)?', '√(a² − x²): x = a sin θ (1 − sin²θ = cos²θ).
√(a² + x²): x = a tan θ (1 + tan²θ = sec²θ).
√(x² − a²): x = a sec θ (sec²θ − 1 = tan²θ).
Each identity collapses the radical into a single trig function.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/partial-fractions-form', 'calc-integration', 14, 'Write the partial fraction form of 1/[(x − 1)²(x² + 4)].', 'A/(x − 1) + B/(x − 1)² + (Cx + D)/(x² + 4).
A repeated linear factor gets one term per power; an irreducible quadratic gets a linear numerator. First make sure deg(top) < deg(bottom); if not, long divide.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-integration/improper-integral-p-test', 'calc-integration', 15, 'For which p does ∫₁^∞ dx/xᵖ converge?', 'p > 1, and then it equals 1/(p − 1).
At p = 1 it''s ln x → ∞, so it diverges. Near 0 the rule flips: ∫₀¹ dx/xᵖ converges only for p < 1.', 'p > 1', array['1<p']::text[])
on conflict (id) do update set pack_id = excluded.pack_id, position = excluded.position, title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, answer = excluded.answer, accepted_answers = excluded.accepted_answers;
delete from public.pack_cards where pack_id = 'calc-integration' and id not in ('calc-integration/ftc-part-2', 'calc-integration/ftc-part-1-chain-rule', 'calc-integration/integral-of-one-over-x', 'calc-integration/arctan-integral-form', 'calc-integration/integral-of-tan', 'calc-integration/u-sub-changing-bounds', 'calc-integration/area-between-curves', 'calc-integration/disk-method', 'calc-integration/shell-method', 'calc-integration/average-value', 'calc-integration/integration-by-parts', 'calc-integration/liate-choice-of-u', 'calc-integration/odd-power-of-sine', 'calc-integration/trig-substitution-table', 'calc-integration/partial-fractions-form', 'calc-integration/improper-integral-p-test');

insert into public.pack_cards (id, pack_id, position, title, body, answer, accepted_answers) values
  ('calc-series/geometric-series-sum', 'calc-series', 0, '∑ₙ₌₁^∞ (2/3)ⁿ = ?', '2.
Geometric with r = 2/3: sum = first term/(1 − r) = (2/3)/(1/3) = 2. Converges only when |r| < 1. Trap: the first term is 2/3, not 1; starting at n = 0 would give 3.', '2', '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-series/divergence-test', 'calc-series', 1, 'Test for Divergence: what does lim aₙ ≠ 0 tell you about ∑ aₙ? What does lim aₙ = 0 tell you?', 'lim aₙ ≠ 0 (or doesn''t exist): ∑ aₙ diverges. lim aₙ = 0: nothing; the series may or may not converge.
The harmonic series ∑ 1/n has terms → 0 yet diverges. The test can prove divergence, never convergence.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-series/p-series', 'calc-series', 2, 'Does ∑ₙ₌₁^∞ 1/√n converge or diverge?', 'Diverges: it''s a p-series with p = 1/2.
∑ 1/nᵖ converges if p > 1 and diverges if p ≤ 1. p = 1 is the harmonic series; ∑ 1/n² (p = 2) converges.', 'diverges', array['diverge', 'divergent', 'it diverges']::text[]),
  ('calc-series/integral-test-conditions', 'calc-series', 3, 'Integral Test: what must f satisfy for ∑ f(n) and ∫₁^∞ f(x) dx to converge or diverge together?', 'f must be continuous, positive and decreasing on [1, ∞) (eventually decreasing is enough).
The integral''s value is not the sum: ∫₁^∞ dx/x² = 1, but ∑ 1/n² = π²/6 ≈ 1.645.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-series/direct-comparison-test', 'calc-series', 4, 'Direct Comparison: 0 ≤ aₙ ≤ bₙ for all n and ∑ bₙ is convergent. What can you conclude about ∑ aₙ?', '∑ aₙ converges (smaller than a convergent series).
If ∑ aₙ diverges, ∑ bₙ diverges too. Smaller than divergent, or bigger than convergent, proves nothing. Example: 1/(n² + 1) < 1/n², so ∑ 1/(n² + 1) converges.', 'converges', array['converge', 'convergent', 'it converges']::text[]),
  ('calc-series/limit-comparison-test', 'calc-series', 5, 'Limit Comparison Test: aₙ, bₙ > 0 and lim aₙ/bₙ = c. For which c do ∑ aₙ and ∑ bₙ share the same fate?', 'Any finite c > 0 (0 < c < ∞): both converge or both diverge.
It rescues cases where direct comparison points the wrong way: 1/(2ⁿ − 1) > 1/2ⁿ, but the ratio → 1 and ∑ 1/2ⁿ converges, so ∑ 1/(2ⁿ − 1) converges.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-series/alternating-series-test', 'calc-series', 6, 'Alternating Series Test: ∑ (−1)ⁿ⁻¹bₙ with bₙ > 0 converges if which two conditions hold?', 'bₙ₊₁ ≤ bₙ (decreasing, at least eventually) and lim bₙ = 0.
Example: ∑ (−1)ⁿ⁻¹/√n converges because 1/√n decreases to 0, even though ∑ 1/√n diverges.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-series/alternating-series-error', 'calc-series', 7, 'Using S₄ to approximate ∑ₙ₌₁^∞ (−1)ⁿ⁻¹/n², what error bound does the Alternating Series Estimation Theorem give?', '1/25.
|S − Sₙ| ≤ bₙ₊₁, the size of the first omitted term; here b₅ = 1/5² = 1/25. It applies when the AST conditions hold (bₙ decreasing to 0).', '1/25', array['0.04', '.04']::text[]),
  ('calc-series/absolute-vs-conditional', 'calc-series', 8, 'The alternating harmonic series ∑ (−1)ⁿ⁻¹/n converges. What kind of convergence is it?', 'Conditional.
Absolute convergence means ∑ |aₙ| converges, and it implies ∑ aₙ converges. Here ∑ |aₙ| = ∑ 1/n diverges, so the convergence is conditional. By contrast, ∑ (−1)ⁿ/n² converges absolutely.', 'conditional', array['conditionally', 'conditionally convergent', 'conditional convergence', 'converges conditionally']::text[]),
  ('calc-series/ratio-test', 'calc-series', 9, 'Ratio Test on ∑ 2ⁿ/n!: what is L = lim |aₙ₊₁/aₙ|?', 'L = 0, so the series converges absolutely.
aₙ₊₁/aₙ = 2/(n + 1) → 0. L < 1: absolute convergence. L > 1 or ∞: divergence. L = 1: inconclusive (∑ 1/n and ∑ 1/n² both give 1). The Root Test, L = lim ⁿ√|aₙ|, has the same verdicts.', '0', array['L = 0']::text[]),
  ('calc-series/interval-of-convergence', 'calc-series', 10, 'Interval of convergence of ∑ₙ₌₁^∞ (x − 3)ⁿ/(n·2ⁿ)?', '[1, 5).
Ratio Test: |x − 3|/2 < 1, so center 3 and radius R = 2. Endpoints need separate checks: x = 5 gives ∑ 1/n (diverges), x = 1 gives ∑ (−1)ⁿ/n (converges by the AST).', '[1,5)', array['1<=x<5', '1≤x<5']::text[]),
  ('calc-series/taylor-coefficient', 'calc-series', 11, 'Taylor series of f centered at a: what is the coefficient cₙ of (x − a)ⁿ?', 'cₙ = f⁽ⁿ⁾(a)/n!, so f(x) = ∑ f⁽ⁿ⁾(a)(x − a)ⁿ/n!. With a = 0 it''s a Maclaurin series.
Check: f(x) = 1/(1 − x) has f⁽ⁿ⁾(0) = n!, so every cₙ = 1, the geometric series ∑ xⁿ (|x| < 1). Trap: forgetting the n!.', 'f^(n)(a)/n!', array['f^n(a)/n!', 'f^(n)(a)/(n!)', '(f^(n)(a))/n!', 'f(n)(a)/n!', 'c_n = f^(n)(a)/n!']::text[]),
  ('calc-series/maclaurin-exp', 'calc-series', 12, 'Maclaurin series: eˣ = ∑ₙ₌₀^∞ ___', 'xⁿ/n! = 1 + x + x²/2! + x³/3! + ..., valid for all x (R = ∞).
At x = 1: e = ∑ 1/n!. Substitute to build others: e^(−x²) = ∑ (−1)ⁿx²ⁿ/n!.', 'x^n/n!', array['x^n/(n!)', '(x^n)/n!', '(1/n!)x^n']::text[]),
  ('calc-series/maclaurin-sin-cos', 'calc-series', 13, 'Maclaurin series for sin x and cos x?', 'sin x = x − x³/3! + x⁵/5! − ... = ∑ (−1)ⁿx²ⁿ⁺¹/(2n + 1)!
cos x = 1 − x²/2! + x⁴/4! − ... = ∑ (−1)ⁿx²ⁿ/(2n)!
Both hold for all x. sin is odd (odd powers), cos is even (even powers), and differentiating sin''s series gives cos''s.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-series/maclaurin-ln', 'calc-series', 14, 'Maclaurin series for ln(1 + x), and for which x does it converge?', 'ln(1 + x) = x − x²/2 + x³/3 − ... = ∑ₙ₌₁^∞ (−1)ⁿ⁻¹xⁿ/n, for −1 < x ≤ 1.
It comes from integrating 1/(1 + x) = ∑ (−1)ⁿxⁿ term by term. At x = 1 it gives ln 2 = 1 − 1/2 + 1/3 − ...', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-series/taylor-inequality', 'calc-series', 15, 'Taylor''s Inequality: if |f⁽ⁿ⁺¹⁾(x)| ≤ M for |x − a| ≤ d, how large can the remainder |Rₙ(x)| be there?', '|Rₙ(x)| ≤ M|x − a|ⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1)!
It looks like the next Taylor term. Lagrange form: Rₙ(x) = f⁽ⁿ⁺¹⁾(z)(x − a)ⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1)! for some z between a and x. Since |x|ⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1)! → 0, eˣ, sin x and cos x equal their series.', null, '{}'::text[])
on conflict (id) do update set pack_id = excluded.pack_id, position = excluded.position, title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, answer = excluded.answer, accepted_answers = excluded.accepted_answers;
delete from public.pack_cards where pack_id = 'calc-series' and id not in ('calc-series/geometric-series-sum', 'calc-series/divergence-test', 'calc-series/p-series', 'calc-series/integral-test-conditions', 'calc-series/direct-comparison-test', 'calc-series/limit-comparison-test', 'calc-series/alternating-series-test', 'calc-series/alternating-series-error', 'calc-series/absolute-vs-conditional', 'calc-series/ratio-test', 'calc-series/interval-of-convergence', 'calc-series/taylor-coefficient', 'calc-series/maclaurin-exp', 'calc-series/maclaurin-sin-cos', 'calc-series/maclaurin-ln', 'calc-series/taylor-inequality');

insert into public.pack_cards (id, pack_id, position, title, body, answer, accepted_answers) values
  ('calc-multivariable/cross-product-magnitude', 'calc-multivariable', 0, 'θ is the angle between vectors a and b. |a × b| = ?', '|a||b| sin θ, the area of the parallelogram spanned by a and b.
a × b is a vector orthogonal to both (right-hand rule); a × b = 0 ⇔ a ∥ b, and b × a = −(a × b). Compare a·b = |a||b| cos θ, a scalar: a·b = 0 ⇔ a ⊥ b.', '|a||b|sin theta', array['|a||b|sin(theta)', '||a|| ||b|| sin theta', '||a|| ||b|| sin(theta)']::text[]),
  ('calc-multivariable/mixed-partials-clairaut', 'calc-multivariable', 1, 'For f(x, y) = x²y³, find f_xy.', '6xy².
f_x = 2xy³, then differentiate in y: f_xy = 6xy². Clairaut''s Theorem: f_yx = (3x²y²)_x = 6xy² as well; mixed partials agree whenever they are continuous.', '6xy^2', array['6y^2x']::text[]),
  ('calc-multivariable/tangent-plane', 'calc-multivariable', 2, 'Equation of the tangent plane to z = x² + y² at (1, 2, 5)?', 'z = 2x + 4y − 5.
Formula: z − z₀ = f_x(a, b)(x − a) + f_y(a, b)(y − b). Here f_x = 2x = 2 and f_y = 2y = 4 at (1, 2), so z − 5 = 2(x − 1) + 4(y − 2).', 'z=2x+4y-5', array['z-5=2(x-1)+4(y-2)', '2x+4y-z=5', '2x+4y-z-5=0', 'z=2(x-1)+4(y-2)+5']::text[]),
  ('calc-multivariable/directional-derivative', 'calc-multivariable', 3, 'f(x, y) = x²y. Find the directional derivative at (1, 3) in the direction of v = ⟨3, 4⟩.', '22/5.
∇f = ⟨2xy, x²⟩ = ⟨6, 1⟩ at (1, 3). Use the unit vector u = ⟨3, 4⟩/5: D_u f = ∇f·u = (18 + 4)/5 = 22/5. Trap: dotting with v itself gives 22.', '22/5', array['4.4']::text[]),
  ('calc-multivariable/gradient-steepest-ascent', 'calc-multivariable', 4, 'At a given point, in which direction does f increase fastest, and what is that fastest rate?', 'Direction ∇f, at rate |∇f|.
D_u f = ∇f·u = |∇f| cos θ, largest at θ = 0. f decreases fastest along −∇f, and D_u f = 0 for u ⊥ ∇f (along the level curve, since ∇f is normal to level curves).', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-multivariable/second-derivative-test-2d', 'calc-multivariable', 5, 'At a critical point of f(x, y), D = f_xx f_yy − (f_xy)² < 0. What kind of point is it?', 'A saddle point.
D > 0 and f_xx > 0: local min. D > 0 and f_xx < 0: local max. D = 0: inconclusive. Example: f = x² − y² at (0, 0) has D = (2)(−2) − 0² = −4.', 'saddle point', array['saddle']::text[]),
  ('calc-multivariable/lagrange-multipliers', 'calc-multivariable', 6, 'Lagrange multipliers: to find extrema of f(x, y) subject to g(x, y) = k, which equations do you solve?', '∇f = λ∇g together with g(x, y) = k (where ∇g ≠ 0).
At a constrained extremum the level curve of f is tangent to the constraint curve, so the gradients are parallel. Evaluate f at every solution: if the extremes exist, the largest value is the max and the smallest the min.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-multivariable/polar-area-element', 'calc-multivariable', 7, 'Converting a double integral to polar coordinates: dA = ?', 'dA = r dr dθ.
A small polar ''rectangle'' is about r Δθ wide and Δr deep. Check: the unit disk''s area is ∫₀^(2π) ∫₀¹ r dr dθ = π. Trap: dropping the r gives 2π.', 'r dr dtheta', array['r dtheta dr']::text[]),
  ('calc-multivariable/spherical-volume-element', 'calc-multivariable', 8, 'Spherical coordinates (ρ, θ, φ): dV = ___ dρ dθ dφ', 'ρ² sin φ.
φ is measured down from the positive z-axis (0 ≤ φ ≤ π). Check: a ball of radius R gives (R³/3)(2π)(2) = (4/3)πR³. Cylindrical for comparison: dV = r dz dr dθ.', 'rho^2 sin phi', array['rho^2 sin(phi)', 'ρ^2 sin φ', 'ρ^2 sin(φ)', 'p^2 sin phi', 'p^2 sin(phi)']::text[]),
  ('calc-multivariable/jacobian-change-of-variables', 'calc-multivariable', 9, 'Change of variables x = g(u, v), y = h(u, v) in a double integral: dA = ?', 'dA = |∂(x, y)/∂(u, v)| du dv, where the Jacobian ∂(x, y)/∂(u, v) = x_u y_v − x_v y_u.
Example: x = 2u + v, y = u − 3v gives (2)(−3) − (1)(1) = −7, so dA = 7 du dv (take the absolute value). For polar coordinates the Jacobian is r.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-multivariable/conservative-field-test', 'calc-multivariable', 10, 'How do you test whether F = ⟨P, Q⟩ is conservative, and what does that buy you?', 'Check ∂P/∂y = ∂Q/∂x (continuous partials on an open, simply connected region); in 3D, check curl F = 0.
Then F = ∇f, so ∫_C F·dr = f(end) − f(start): path-independent, and 0 around closed curves. Example: ⟨2xy, x²⟩ = ∇(x²y).', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-multivariable/greens-theorem', 'calc-multivariable', 11, 'Green''s Theorem: ∮_C P dx + Q dy = ∬_D (___) dA for positively oriented C. What goes in the blank?', '∂Q/∂x − ∂P/∂y.
C is a counterclockwise, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve bounding D. Area shortcut: A = ∮ x dy = ½∮ (x dy − y dx). Trap: P_y − Q_x flips the sign.', 'Q_x - P_y', array['Qx-Py', 'dQ/dx-dP/dy', '∂Q/∂x-∂P/∂y', '-P_y+Q_x']::text[]),
  ('calc-multivariable/div-and-curl', 'calc-multivariable', 12, 'For F = ⟨P, Q, R⟩, what are div F and curl F?', 'div F = ∇·F = P_x + Q_y + R_z, a scalar. curl F = ∇ × F = ⟨R_y − Q_z, P_z − R_x, Q_x − P_y⟩, a vector.
Identities (continuous second partials): curl(∇f) = 0 and div(curl F) = 0.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-multivariable/stokes-theorem', 'calc-multivariable', 13, 'Stokes'' Theorem: ∮_C F·dr equals what surface integral, and how are C and S oriented?', '∬_S curl F·dS, where C is the boundary of the oriented surface S.
Right-hand rule: thumb along S''s normal, fingers curl in C''s direction (walk along C with your head along the normal and S stays on your left). Green''s Theorem is the flat special case.', null, '{}'::text[]),
  ('calc-multivariable/divergence-theorem', 'calc-multivariable', 14, 'Outward flux of F = ⟨x, y, z⟩ through the unit sphere?', '4π.
Divergence Theorem: ∬_S F·dS = ∭_E div F dV for a closed surface S oriented outward around solid E. Here div F = 3, so the flux is 3·(4/3)π = 4π.', '4pi', '{}'::text[])
on conflict (id) do update set pack_id = excluded.pack_id, position = excluded.position, title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, answer = excluded.answer, accepted_answers = excluded.accepted_answers;
